causa.</ITEM> <ITEM>Massan <NOTE N="h" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0781" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0781B"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Masan.</HI>  In editis scriptum est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Mabram,</HI>  quod
nomen non legitur in Genesi: unde corruptum intelligitur,
et  <HI REND="ITALIC">Mabram</HI>  esse positum pro  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230781H" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Mabsam,</HI> 
Gen. XXV, 13, sive potius pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Massaúm,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Maffam,</HI> 
ut legimus apud LXX et in codicibus mss. Hieronymus
enim hunc librum edidit post Philonem Judaeum,
qui utebatur Versione Graeca LXX Translatorum.
 <COL N="0782A"></COL> Verum ex posita significatione ac etymologia
legendum ut in Hebraico,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Mabsan,</HI>  quia illud nomen
constructum figuratur ex servili  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230782C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Mem,</HI>  et verbo
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230782C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">basam</HI>  juxta quod significat,  <HI REND="ITALIC">de jucunditatibus.</HI> 
 <ED>MARTIAN.</ED> —Noster ms. cum vulgo editis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Mabram,</HI> 
de qua corrupta lectione Martianaeus disserit.</NOTE> , de jucunditatibus.</ITEM> <ITEM>Masmae, exaudientem.</ITEM> <ITEM>Massa, levans, sive pondus, aut
