 <COL N="0783"></COL> legerimus: exin per extensum
pronuntiemus elementum.</HI></ITEM> <ITEM>Ophir ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ) <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0783" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0783A"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Ophir, sive Uphir.</HI>  In editis libris et aliquot mss.
 <HI REND="ITALIC">sive Aphir.</HI>  Sed error manifestus est librariorum veterum,
qui legentes,  <HI REND="ITALIC">quod per Aleph scribitur,</HI>  putarunt
Hieronymum posuisse,  <HI REND="ITALIC">sive Aphir;</HI>  cum ex fide
codicum mss. scripserit,  <HI REND="ITALIC">sive Uphir.</HI>  Aleph enim
quiescit in hoc nomine  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , et  <HI REND="ITALIC">Vau</HI>  sequens initio
nominis auditur, sive illud  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  legatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">o</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">u; Ophir,</HI> 
vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">Uphir.</HI>   <ED>MARTIAN.</ED> —Etiam in ms. nostro  <HI REND="ITALIC">sive
Aphir,</HI>  quemadmodum in aliis Martianaeus invenit,
et vulgati libri praeferebant. Vid. quae ipse editor
Benedictinus annotavit.</NOTE> , sive Uphir, quod
per ALEPH litteram scribitur, interpretatur
irritum, vel inherbosum.</ITEM> <ITEM>Ox <NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0783" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0783A"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Ox, volentem.</HI>  Multis diversisque modis nomen
istud mutatur in Graecis ac Latinis exemplaribus, est
enim Hebraeum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783E" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Hus;</HI>  quod Septuaginta Interpretes
legebant  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>wßéû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Hos,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Os,</HI>  Genes. XXXVI, 28.
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>OuÞû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Ous,</HI>  autem sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Us,</HI>  Gen. XXII, 21. Nomen
itaque  <HI REND="ITALIC">Os</HI>  Latini mutavere cum  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ox,</HI>  frequenter enim
 <HI REND="ITALIC">x</HI>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">s</HI>  usurpatur ab antiquis librariis, ut in Jobi
libro secundum LXX,  <HI REND="ITALIC">in regione Auxitidi,</HI>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ausitidi.</HI> 
Septuaginta quoque cum elemento  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>x</FOREIGN>  nonnulla
 <COL N="0783B"></COL> Hebraeorum vocabula scripta retinent, ut  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Bauúx,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Baux,</HI> 
ex Hebraico  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Buz.</HI>  Unum est, quod nostris observationibus
obsistere possit, significatio nempe vocis
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>wßû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Os</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ox,</HI>  quae transfertur hoc loco in
 <HI REND="ITALIC">volentem,</HI>  cum tamen significet  <HI REND="ITALIC">consiliatorem,</HI>  etiam
apud Hieronymum infra in verbo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Us.</HI>  Sed nisi me fallat
opinio, posuerat Hieronymus Graecam vocem  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>bouleuthún</FOREIGN> ;
sicut in nomine  <HI REND="ITALIC">Chanaan</HI>  vocabulum Graecum
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>savloû,</FOREIGN>  et in  <HI REND="ITALIC">Agag,</HI>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>dwðma,</FOREIGN>  etc., quod minime intellectum
ab imperitis librariis, pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>bouleuthún</FOREIGN>  posuerunt  <HI REND="ITALIC">volentem.</HI> 
Judith. VIII, 1, apud LXX,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Wx,</FOREIGN>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Idex.</HI>   <ED>MARTIAN.</ED> —Satis
ingeniose Martianaeus pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">volentem,</HI>  scriptum
conjicit abs Hieronymo, ut alias subinde occurrit,
litteris Graecis  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>bouleuthún,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Bouleuten,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">consiliatorem.</HI> 
Haec nempe hujus nominis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ox,</HI>  seu potius
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Hus,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">Hutz,</HI>  filii Aram, Genes. XIX, 23, propria
est expositio. Hebraice  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783G" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  scribitur, Septuaginta
autem Graece  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>8Wû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Hos,</HI>  verterunt.</NOTE> , volentem,</ITEM> <ITEM>Omar, populus, vel amarus.</ITEM> <ITEM>Onan <NOTE N="c" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0783" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0783B"></COL> Origenes in Joannem  <HI REND="ITALIC">labor eorum</HI>  interpretatur,
 <COL N="0783C"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Wnaún ejsti povnoû aujtwðn.</FOREIGN>  Ab  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783H" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , cum affixo plurali
feminino.</NOTE> , moeror eorum.</ITEM> <CAPTION COM="0783">R.</CAPTION> <ITEM><HI REND=BOLD>15</HI>  Ripheth, videns buccellam,
sive videns bona.</ITEM> <ITEM>Rhodii, quod Hebraice dicitur
RODIM vel Rodanim  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  [ <HI REND="ITALIC">In Hebr.</HI> 
Dodanim  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ], interpretantur
descendentes, aut patruus judicans,
vel visio judicii.</ITEM> <ITEM>Raama ( <HI REND="ITALIC">Rhegma</HI> ) <NOTE N="d" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0784" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0784A"></COL> Ms. noster,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Rechema tonans, vel excelsa.</HI></NOTE> , tonitruum,
vel excelsa.</ITEM> <ITEM>Rhooboth, inclinatio, vel plateae.</ITEM> <ITEM>Ragau, aegrotans, vel pascens.</ITEM> <ITEM>Raphaim, gigantes.</ITEM> <ITEM>Rebecca <NOTE N="e" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0784" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0784A"></COL> Deduci puta ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784E" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , vel  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784E" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , quod significat
 <HI REND="ITALIC">multum esse, multiplicari,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784E" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">exspectavit, sustinuit.</HI> 
Consentit Origenes homil. 12 in Numeros,
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Invenit Rebeccam, quae interpretatur patientia.</HI>  Attamen
longius petita etymologia est.</NOTE> , multa patientia, vel
multum accepit, sive patientia.</ITEM> <ITEM>Remma, vel Ruma  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , videns
aliquid, vel excelsa.</ITEM> <ITEM>Rachel <NOTE N="f" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0784" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0784A"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Rachel, ovis,</HI>  etc.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Rachel</HI>  significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">ovem,</HI>  cum
Hebraice media littera est  <HI REND="ITALIC">hheth,</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Rahhel,</HI>  sive
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Rachel.</HI>  Si autem figuratum fuerit ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">raa,</HI>  et
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">el,</HI>  sonat  <HI REND="ITALIC">videntem Deum.</HI>  Rursus ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">raa,</HI> 
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">hhalal,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Chalal, videntem principium.</HI>  Denique
figuratum ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">rai,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">ra</HI>  cum Ain,  <HI REND="ITALIC">visionem</HI> 
ac  <HI REND="ITALIC">scelus</HI>  significat, sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">visionem sceleris.</HI> 
Hanc igitur litterarum diversitatem intelligit S. Hieronymus,
ut et accentuum in vocibus  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,
id est, in  <HI REND="ITALIC">ra</HI>  cum Aleph, vel in  <HI REND="ITALIC">ra</HI>  cum Heth, aut in  <HI REND="ITALIC">ra</HI> 
cum Ain.  <ED>MARTIAN.</ED> —Hebraice  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  scribitur, atque
 <HI REND="ITALIC">agnam,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">oviculam,</HI>  ex etymo  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784F" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">mola arietis,</HI> 
 <COL N="0784B"></COL> significat. Reliquae interpretationes, quae paulo violentiores
sunt, quemadmodum extundi possint, Martianaeus
docet.</NOTE> , ovis, vel videns principium,
aut visio sceleris, sive videns
Deum. Hoc autem secundum
accentuum et litterarum evenit
diversitatem, ut tam in contrarias
significationes nomina commutentur.</ITEM> <ITEM>Ruben <NOTE N="g" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0784" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0784B"></COL> Origenes ferme elegantius modo passivo  <HI REND="ITALIC">filius
qui videtur, hoc est visibilis.</HI>  Homil. 17 in Genes.
Obvia radix  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784G" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">vidit</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784G" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">filius.</HI></NOTE> , videns filius, vel videns
 <COL N="0783"></COL> in medio.</ITEM> <ITEM>Raguel, pastor Dei, vel pabulum
ejus Deus.</ITEM> <ITEM>Ros, caput.</ITEM> <ITEM>Ramesse ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783I" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ), pabulum,
 <NOTE N="h" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="0784" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="0784B"></COL> Malim hic quoque  <HI REND="ITALIC">de tinea</HI>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">vel tinea.</HI>  Vide
quae annotavimus de hoc nomine in Epist. 78 de
Mansionibus ad Fabiolam Mans. I. Origenes homil. 1
in Exod.,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ramesses,</HI>  inquit,  <HI REND="ITALIC">quae interpretatur commotio
tinea:</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784H" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">tinea.</HI></NOTE>  vel tinea, sive malitia de tinea.</ITEM> <ITEM>Quod in principio dixeramus in
vocalibus litteris observandum: eo
quod apud nos interdum una sit littera,
et apud Hebraeos variis vocibus
proferatur: hoc nunc quoque in
S littera sciendum est. Siquidem
apud Hebraeos tres sunt S litterae:
una quae dicitur SAMECH ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783J" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ),
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783J" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , et simpliciter legitur, quasi per
S nostram litteram describatur:
alia SIN ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783J" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ),  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783J" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , in qua stridor
quidam non nostri sermonis interstrepit:
tertia SADE ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783J" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ),  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230783J" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,
quam nostrae aures penitus reformidant.
Sicubi ergo evenerit, ut
eadem nomina aliter atque aliter
interpretentur, illud in causa est,
quod diversis scripta sunt litteris.
Hoc autem quod in Genesi diximus,
in omnibus libris similiter observandum.</ITEM> <CAPTION COM="0783">S.</CAPTION> <ITEM><HI REND=BOLD>16</HI>  Sela, umbra ejus.</ITEM> <ITEM>Seht, positio, vel positus, aut
poculum, sive gramen, aut semen,
seu resurrectio.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sem, nomen, vel nominatus.</ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="0784"></COL> Saba ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ), captus sive captivitas,
vel certe convertens.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sabatha, gyrans, sive circumiens.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sabathacha, circumiens te, sive
circumsessio tua, vel sedes tua.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sennaar, excussio dentium, sive
fetor eorum.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sidona ( <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230784B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ), venatio moeroris.</ITEM> <ITEM>Samaraeum, lanam meam, sive
conjunctum meum, sed melius
custos.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sodoma [al.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Sodomorum</HI> ], pecus,
silens, vel caecitas, vel similitudo
