 <HI REND="ITALIC">Esau</HI>  vertitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">rubeus,</HI>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">roboreus,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">robustus.</HI> 
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230855A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Esau</HI>  enim vertitur in  <HI REND="ITALIC">robustum;</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230855A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Edom</HI> 
vero in  <HI REND="ITALIC">rubeum,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">rufum.</HI>  Vide infra in Graecis
Fragmentis vocem  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>druvinoû,</FOREIGN>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">roboreus.</HI> 
 <ED>MARTIAN.</ED> 

—Iidem libri corrupte  <HI REND="ITALIC">rubeum,</HI>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">roboreum</HI>  legunt.
Graecis quoque  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>druviçnoû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">roboreus,</HI>  ab arbore  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>druðû,</FOREIGN> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">robur, quercus</HI>  appellatur Esau. Re autem ipsa non
ab arbore deducenda etymologia haec est, sed a fortitudine,
ex Hebraico verbo  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0230855B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">robur, validae vires,</HI> 
etc.</NOTE>  roboreus, sive acervus
