 <HI REND="ITALIC">cum etymologia nominis Aser Scripturae auctoritate
pandatur,</HI>  etc.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aser ergo non divitiae, sed beatus dicitur,</HI> 
etc.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>plouðtoû, hÞ makavrioû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iwshúf, ojneivdou ajfaivresiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Beniamiún, tevknon ojduvnhû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Rebevkka, carav.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Rachúl, provbaton.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Klivmax</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1147" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1147A"></COL> Nomen  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Klivmax</FOREIGN>  cum tribus sequentibus,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Marsivppion</FOREIGN> 
scilicet,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ophlovû,</FOREIGN>  et  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Plivnqw,</FOREIGN>  nullatenus pertinent ad
sermonem Hebraeum; sed purae sunt Graecae dictiones,
quae apud vulgus Graecorum usurpari potuere
proverbialiter juxta sensum verbis singulis affixum,
ita ut  <HI REND="ITALIC">sermo Dei</HI>  per metaphoram sit  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Klivmax,</FOREIGN>  id est,
 <HI REND="ITALIC">scala,</HI>  Gen. XXVIII, 12, per quam ascendunt homines
ad Deum.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Marsivppion</FOREIGN>  vero, sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Marsupium,</HI>  Proverb.
I, 14,  <HI REND="ITALIC">consensum</HI>  significet secundum adagium
illud,  <HI REND="ITALIC">pecuniae obediunt omnia.</HI>  Denique duobus aliis
vocabulis vitia intemperantiae coarguuntur, quae in
typo praecesserunt in operibus  <HI REND="ITALIC">luti,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">lateris,</HI>  quibus
Aegyptii serviliter Hebraeos aliquando premebant,
Exodi I, 14. Non omitto  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Marsivppion</FOREIGN>  fol. 41 codicis
sms. Regii aliter interpretatum, nempe:  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Marsivppion,
 <COL N="1147B"></COL> balavntion,</FOREIGN>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Marsupium, crumena.</HI></NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>oJ lovgoû touð Qeouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sepfovra, caraú kaiú kavlloû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Marsivppion, toú peivqesqai.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Ophloúû, gastrimargiva.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Plivnqwé, thðé porneivaé.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Bhqleeúm</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1147" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1147B"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Bethleem</HI>  nomen juxta proprietatem linguae Hebraicae
interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">domus panis,</HI>  aliae etymologiae
a Graecis sunt copulatae propter Christum Dominum
in Bethleem natum. Subit autem animo haud mediocris
 <COL N="1148A"></COL> admiratio, cur nomen Bethleem praetermissum
sit ab Hieronymo in libro Hebraicorum Nominum,
etsi legatur in omnibus fere voluminibus veteris ac
novi Testamenti. Forte id causae est, quod ipse
indicat lib. Quaest. Hebr. in Genesim:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ephrata vero
et Bethleem,</HI>  inquit,  <HI REND="ITALIC">unius urbis vocabulum est, sub
interpretatione consimili. Siquidem in</HI>  frugiferam,  <HI REND="ITALIC">et
in</HI>  domum panis  <HI REND="ITALIC">vertitur, propter eum panem, qui de
coelo se descendisse dicit.</HI>  Non recenset igitur nomen
Bethleem, quia Ephratam saepius interpretatur.</NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>oiøkoû zwhðû, hÞ oiøkoû aJgivou,
hÞ oiøkoû aærtou.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Bhqfaghð, oiøkoû ejpitucivaû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Bhqaniva, oiøkoû dovxhû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Bhrsabeeú, qugavthr dunatouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Savra, ajrcomevnh.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>SavrjrJa</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1147" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1147B"></COL> Ita cum duplici  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>r</FOREIGN>  alterum  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>SavrjrJa</FOREIGN>  legit Vatican.
