 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iawð,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Deus,</HI>  quod nomen frequentissime usurpant
Graeci, et cum primis Origenes, ipsumque est Hebraicum
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , quod Samaritae per  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iabaiú,</FOREIGN>  Eusebius
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ieunwú,</FOREIGN>  Theodoritus  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajiçaú</FOREIGN>  (fort,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ijaiçaú</FOREIGN> ) efferunt. Hebraica
nomina, quae litteris  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  componuntur, solenne
 <COL N="1152B"></COL> Adamantio fuit per  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Iawú</FOREIGN>  interpretari. Ita ex.
gr.  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231152E" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  vertit  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>metewrismoún 1Iawú,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">sublimitatem Iao,</HI> 
nihilque adeo propius ad fidem est, quam hic ipsum
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iwð,</FOREIGN>  sive  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iawð</FOREIGN>  significari. Vide quae annotavimus ad
nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Joannam</HI>  penes Hieronymum pag. 91.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ijwð, oå ejsti cavriû qeouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Andrevaû, duvnamiû eujprephúû, hÞ ajpokrinovmenoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iavkwboû 1Alfaiouð, pternismoúû maqhvsewû zwhðû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iavkwboû, pternisthúû povnou.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Fivlippoû, stovma ceirwðn, hÞ stovma
lampavdoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Barqolomaiðoû, uiJoúû kremavsaû uådata.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qwmaðû, aæbussoû, hÞ divdumoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ananivaû, doxavzwn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Azarivaû, blevpwn levwn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Mhsahúl, pwðû</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1151" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1151A"></COL> Melius, ni fallor, legeretur in Graeco  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnistaðé,</FOREIGN> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">resurgat,</HI>  vel quid simile. Latina porro Hieronymi
restituimus ad fidem codicum mss. Gemeticensis
monasterii, Fiscanensis, S. Ebrulphi Uticensis, et
S. Martini Sagiensis, qui omnes retinent genuinam
lectionem,  <HI REND="ITALIC">quae est salus Dei?</HI>  Editi autem cum mss.
Exemplaribus, Regio uno, duobus Colbertinis, et
cum aliis pluribus depravate legunt,  <HI REND="ITALIC">quae palus Domini?</HI> 
Neque vero ex nomine  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Misael,</HI>  paludis
significatio elici potest, sed salutis; ita tamen
ut nomen figuratum intelligatur ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">mi,</HI> 
id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">quae,</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">jes, est,</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">jasa, salvavit,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231151A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,
 <HI REND="ITALIC">el, Deus.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnistaðé</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1152" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1152B"></COL> Nimirum ita Vatic. emendat, quemadmodum
Martianaeus conjecerat, qui tamen retinuit ex Regio
cod.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnistaú,</FOREIGN>  et Latin.  <HI REND="ITALIC">resurrexit,</HI>  mendose. Vide quae
ad hoc nomen penes Hieron. annotamus.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>oJ qeovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Efouúd</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1151" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1151A"></COL> Nulla est etymologia nominis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ephod</HI>  in Graeco,
vel in Latina interpretatione nostra.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ephod</HI>  namque
non significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">manifestationem,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">veritatem;</HI>  sed
 <COL N="1152A"></COL> in Rationali Judicii positum erat, sive scriptum
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231152A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">haurim vehattumim,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">doctrina
et veritas,</HI>  Exodi XXVIII, 30. Hinc Graeci linguae
Hebraicae imperiti aestimabant Ephod significare
 <HI REND="ITALIC">manifestationem et veritatem.</HI></NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>dhvlwsiû, kaiú ajlhvqeia.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ihsouðû, swthúr.</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1152" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1152A"></COL> Lege  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnuovmenoû,</FOREIGN>  ab  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnuvomai,</FOREIGN>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">perficior,</HI> 
Jesus igitur Latine dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">Salvator;</HI>  vox  <HI REND="ITALIC">perfectus</HI> 
a Graecis addua est.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajniouvmenoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Hliouð, ijscurouð</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1152" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1152A"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aujqevnthû</FOREIGN>  est qui pro arbitrio et auctoritate sua
quidvis agendi potestatem habet; quo sensu Dominus
omnium Deus vere dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">authentes,</HI>  sive
 <HI REND="ITALIC">ischyros authentes,</HI>  quasi dicas invictissimum et omnipotentem.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>aujqevntou.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Elissaiðoû, qeouð duvnamiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="5" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1152" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1152A"></COL> Nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zachaei</HI>  ex usu linguae Syriacae significat
 <HI REND="ITALIC">justum,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">mundum,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">victorem.</HI>  Unde  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231152B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">tizke,</HI>  psal. L, intelligitur,  <HI REND="ITALIC">mundus sis,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">vincas.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zakcaiðrû, nikhthvû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Kavdhû, aJgiasmovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Kavdwn, aJgivaû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sivgwr, tapeivnwsiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="6" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1152" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1152A"></COL> Sion juxta proprietatem linguae Hebraicae non
 <COL N="1153A"></COL> possumus interpretari  <HI REND="ITALIC">domum spiritus.</HI>  Symbolice
igitur haec accipienda sunt, quia mous Sion typus
est Ecclesiae Christi, quae inhabitantem ac praesidem
habet Spiritum sanctum.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Siwún, skopeuthvrion, hÞ oiøkoû touð
pneumavtoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="1153"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aarwún, oæroû ijscuvoû,</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Ierousalhúm, oårasiû eijrhvnhû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Babulwún, suvgcusiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Basaún, aijscuvnh.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>5Wg</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1153" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1153B"></COL> Vitiose Martianaeus nomen  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>5Wn,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">On</HI>  substituerat
pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Wg,</FOREIGN>  et in Hieronymiano quoque laterculo  <HI REND="ITALIC">On,
labor, vel dolor.</HI>  Nedum vero Vaticani libri auctorias,
ipsa etiam hujus nominis interpretatio  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diavfraxiû,</FOREIGN> 
evincit, regis Og nomen hic designari; cui
facile ex Graecorum sensu  <HI REND="ITALIC">intersepti</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">interclusionis</HI> 
quadrat etymologia; minime vero omnium voci  <HI REND="ITALIC">On,</HI> 
ex qua nihil simile valeas extorquere. Origenes
Homil. 13 in Numer.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Og,</HI>  inquit, interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">interclusio.</HI></NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diavfraxiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adonibezeúk</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1153" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1153A"></COL> Nomen istud interpretantur Graeci ac si legeretur
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Adoniezer,</HI>  quod merito verterent,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Domini auxilium,</HI> 
ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231153A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">adon</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231153A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ezer.</HI>  At de vocabulo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Adonibezec,</HI> 
consule supra Annotatiunculas nostras in
libri Judicum nomina.</NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>kurivou bohvqeia.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Dabiúd, uiJoúû ajgaphtovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Solovmon, ejlehvmwn,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1153" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1153A"></COL> Quod proprium est Davidi. Graeci exscriptores
filio ejus Salomoni adscribunt imperite; non enim
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Salomon,</HI>  sed  <HI REND="ITALIC">David</HI>  interpretatur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>iJkanoúû ceiriú,</FOREIGN>  id
est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">fortis manu,</HI>  ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231153B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">dai,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231153B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">jad.</HI>  Econtrario
Salomon  <HI REND="ITALIC">filius dilectus</HI>  patiori jure diceretur, quam
David; nam alio nomine Salomon appellatus est
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Ididia,</HI>  quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">dilectum Domini</HI>  sonat. Verumtamen
nec David, nec Salomon, id nominis habere possunt
juxta proprietatem sermonis Hebraei, aut juxta
fidem elementorum; sed quatenus sunt imagines et
symbola veri Davidis, ac pacifici Salomonis, id est,
 <COL N="1153B"></COL> Christi Domini, qui a Deo patre dictus est  <HI REND="ITALIC">filius dilectissimus.</HI> 
Idem esto judicium de reliquis vocabulis
subsequentibus, quae omnia fere secundum typicas
et symbolicas explicationes posita leguntur; non
secundum etymologiam linguae Hebraicae.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>hÞ iJkanoúû ceiriú, hÞ
eijrhnikovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Rachúl, pnohú aænwqen ijscuraæ.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Rebevkka, pnohú aænwqen.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Cananaiðoi, proskunouðnteû ajnomivan.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Cettaiðoi</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1153" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1153B"></COL> Hieronymus passive sumpsit nomen  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231153C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hhiththim</HI> 
sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Chetthim,</HI>  a radice  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231153C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hhathath,</HI>  quae sensum
 <COL N="1154A"></COL> habet passivum in Kal, et significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">territus et consternatus
fuit:</HI>  in conjugatione autem Hiphil, activam
habet significationem, quam Graeci sequuntur addentes
insuper verbum  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajnovmwn</FOREIGN>  ad etymologiam nominis
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Chettim.</HI></NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejxistwðnteû ajnovmwn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1AmorjrJaiðoi, pikraivnonteû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Feraizaiðri, skorpivzontai.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Eujaiðoi</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1153" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1153C"></COL> Insigni depravatione deerat isthaec Evaei nominis
expositio,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>skolioiú wjû ejpiú oæfeiû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">maligni, sive tortuosi,
quasi serpentes:</HI>  nam pro ea, quae subsequitur,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Gergesaiðoi</FOREIGN> 
 <COL N="1154B"></COL> voce, pessime substitutum erat  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Eujaiðoi katoivkhsiû</FOREIGN> 
etc. et quod Gergesaeorum erat etymon, Evaeis tribuebatur.
Qua de re Martianaeus lacunam in codice Regio
non animadvertens, ita hariolando disserebat:
Evaei,  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231154A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Evim</HI>  dicuntur  <HI REND="ITALIC">pessimi et feri</HI>  ab  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231154A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ava,</HI> 
hoc est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">inique egit. Domicilium</HI>  autem  <HI REND="ITALIC">vanum</HI>  interpretari
possunt, si violenter nomen deducas ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231154A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">aun,</HI>  unde  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231154A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">maon,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">habitatio et habitaculum,</HI> 
 <COL N="1154C"></COL> et etiam ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231154A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">aven</HI>  cum aleph ab initio, quod significat
 <HI REND="ITALIC">vanitatem.</HI>  Nos ex Vatic. archetypo locum supplevimus
ac restituimus. Vide idem hoc nomen in IV
Fragmento.</NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>skolioiú wJû ejpiú oåfeiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Gergesaiðoi, katoivkhsiû mataiva.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Iebousaiðoi, kataiscunovmenoi.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Feiswún</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1154" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1154A"></COL> Miror Graecorum subtilitatem in confingendis
nominum Hebraicorum etymologiis; cum utique
Phison et Geon utcumque accipiant, non Graecam,
sed Hebraeam debeant habere rationem, quia ipsa
nomina Hebraica sunt. Nemo autem in altera lingua
quidpiam vocans, etymologiam vocabuli sumit ex
altera. At Graecorum libertati parcamus, qui a Philone
disertissimo Judaeorum scriptore etymologias id
genus accepisse noscuntur. Libro namque Legis Allegortarum
de quatuor Paradisi fluminibus disserens,
haec super nomine  <HI REND="ITALIC">Phison</HI>  ab eo observata leguntur
pag. 52:  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Twðn,</FOREIGN>  inquit,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>tettavrwn ajretwðn eån eiødovû ejstin hJ
frovnhsiû, hÝn fuswún wjnovmase paraú toú feivdesqai kaiú fulavttein
thún yuchún ajpoú ajdikhmavtwn.</FOREIGN>  Id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">E quatuor virtutibus
una species est prudentia, quam Physon nominavit,
 <COL N="1154B"></COL> eo quod parcat, et servet animam a laesione et injuria.</HI> 
Bene igitur Graecis convenit cum Philone in allegorica
interpretatione nominis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Phison,</HI>  quod dictum ita
volunt  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpoú touð feivdesqai,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">a parcendo,</HI>  atque  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpoú touð
fulavttein,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">a servando.</HI>  Deus autem tunc maxime parcit
humanae conditioni ac fragilitati, cum animam
servat a laesione et injuria peccati. Sed moralia sunt
 <COL N="1155A"></COL> isthaec, nec satis accurate scripta in Nominum Hebraicorum
interpretatione, quae significantiam postulat
elementorum, non peregrinam allegoriarum diffusionem.
Graecis itaque relictis, Hieronymo nostro
toti adhaereamus, utpote qui non minus accurate
quam docte dictionum Hebraicarum etymologias proponat.</NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diaú toú feivsasqai toún qeoún
thðû ajnqrwpovthtoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="1155"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Geivwn, oåti ajpoú thðû ghðû eæplasen oJ
qeoúû toún aænqrwpon.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1155" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1155A"></COL> Nominis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Tigris</HI>  non meminit Hieronymus, quia
Graecum est ac Hebraeis ignotum; fluvius enim tertius
Hebraice nominatur  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231155A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hhiddecel,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">adacel,</HI> 
quod interpretari possumus  <HI REND="ITALIC">acutum et celerem.</HI>  Hujus
porro fluvii aquae dicuntur  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231155A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">haddim,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">acutae,</HI> 
et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231155A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Calim,</HI>  hoc est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">leves,</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">celeres.</HI>  Corrupto
vocabulo Chaldaeis dicitur  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231155A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">diglath.</HI>  Hinc apud
Josephum lib. I Antiqq. cap. 2:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Tigris autem Diglath,
quod interpretatur angustus simul et velox.</HI>  Caeterum
Graecis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Tigris,</HI>  idem est quod Persis et Chaldaeis
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231155B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">gir,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">sagitta,</HI>  cujus velocitatem aliquo
modo videtur imitatus hic fluvius. De eodem fluvio
sic legimus apud Hesychium:  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Tivgrhû, potamoúû Perswðn×
 <COL N="1155B"></COL> kaiú oJ touvtou potamouð rJoiðzoû, kaiú zwðon, paraú meún 1Ioudaivoiû
foravdei, paraú deú 4Ellhsi Tivgriû.</FOREIGN>  Id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Tigres, fluvius
Persarum: et ipsius fluvii impetus, et animal, Judaeis
quidem Phoradei, Graecis vero Tigris.</HI>  Locus tenebrosus,
et nisi me fallat opinio, non uno modo corruptus.
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Tigris</HI>  Graecis est animal quod apud Judaeos dicitur
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Phoradei.</HI>  Hoc sonare videntur Hesychii verba
Graeca. Sed  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231155C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Phiradim,</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">Phoradeim</HI>  Judaeis
 <COL N="1156A"></COL> sunt  <HI REND="ITALIC">muli,</HI>  non  <HI REND="ITALIC">tigrides.</HI>  Dicamus igitur Hesychium
confundere Tigrim fluvium cum Euphrate, sicut supra
Seraphim cum Cherubim, et pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>foravdei</FOREIGN>  legendum
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>foravqei,</FOREIGN>  aut  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>foravthû,</FOREIGN>  quod ipsum erit Judaeorum
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231156A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Pherath,</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">Phorathe.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Tivgriû, pivsteuma, oJxuvthtoû</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1155" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1155B"></COL> Erudite contendit Clericus reponendum  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>rJeuðma,</FOREIGN> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">fluentum,</HI>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pneuðma,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">spiritus.</HI>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Oxuvthtoû</FOREIGN>  quoque Latin
 <COL N="1156B"></COL> vertendom celeritatis, non  <HI REND="ITALIC">acuminis.</HI>  Typographor.
oscitantia isthaec suo loco annotatio decidit.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pneuðma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Eujfravthû, aujqevnthû, fwtivzontoû
pneuðma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1156" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1156A"></COL> Ex hoc loco iterum coarguendus error veterum
editionum, ubi legimus  <HI REND="ITALIC">Osanna</HI>  Graece interpretari
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>wJsannaú,</FOREIGN>  quod perinde est ac si diceremus  <HI REND="ITALIC">librum</HI>  sonare
 <HI REND="ITALIC">librum. Osanna</HI>  ergo in linguam Graecam vertitur
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>swðson dhú,</FOREIGN>  quod Latine est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">salvifica.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Wsannaú, swðson dhú, hÞ swvsantoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Enwúc, ejgkainismovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>4Iessai, ajnavyuxiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aiæguptoû</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1156" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1156A"></COL> Quia Aegyptus locus fuerat afflictionis Hebraeorum
multis diversisque tribulationibus ac injuriis
ibi oppressorum, ideo a Graecis interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">angustia,</HI> 
sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">tribulatio.</HI>  Similiter propter illas tenebras
horribiles, quibus terra Aegypti obtecta est tempore
Mosis,  <HI REND="ITALIC">caligo</HI>  dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aegyptus.</HI></NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>qlivyiû, hÞ skovtoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Diavboloû</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1156" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1156A"></COL> Diabolus,  <HI REND="ITALIC">defluens</HI>  interpretatur ab Hieronymo,
quia Hebraeis  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231156B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">zub,</HI>  significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">fluere;</HI>  apud Aeoles
autem  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diavboloû</FOREIGN>  scribitur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>zavbouloû</FOREIGN> ; unde  <HI REND="ITALIC">diabolus,</HI> 
seu potius  <HI REND="ITALIC">zaboulos</HI>  facile  <HI REND="ITALIC">defluens</HI>  interpretatur. Affligens,
 <COL N="1156B"></COL> et temporarius dicitur a Graecis, non juxta
fidem elementorum, sed propter malignitatem tentationum
ejus quas Deus non sinit esse continuas. Hinc
de illo scriptum legimus Luc IV., 13:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Et consummata
omni tentatione, diabolus recessit ab illo, usque ad
tempus,</HI>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>aæcri kairouð,</FOREIGN>  unde nominatur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>provskairoû</FOREIGN> ; id
est  <HI REND="ITALIC">temporarius.</HI></NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>qlivbwn, hÞ provskairoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abbaú, oJ pathvr.</FOREIGN></ITEM></LIST></DIV2> <DIV2 SECOND=YES COM="1155"><HEAD COM="1155"><HI REND="ITALIC">Hieronymus.
LIBER NOMINUM Hebraicorum
Scripturae sacrae.</HI></HEAD> <COL N="1146"></COL> <LIST COM="1146"><ITEM>Adam, homo, sive terrenus, aut
indigena, vel terra rubra.</ITEM> <ITEM>Abel, luctus, sive vanitas, vel vapor,
aut miserabilis.</ITEM> <ITEM>Cain, possessio, vel lamentatio.</ITEM> <ITEM>Abraham, pater videns populum.</ITEM> <ITEM>Abram, pater excelsus.</ITEM> <ITEM>Ruben, videns filius, vel videns in
medio.</ITEM> <ITEM>Simeon, exauditio, vel nomen habitaculi.</ITEM> <ITEM>Levi, additus, sive assumptus.</ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="1148"></COL> Judas, laudatio, sive confessio.</ITEM> <ITEM>Issachar, est merces.</ITEM> <ITEM>Zabulon, habitaculum eorum, etc.,
vel fluxus noctis.</ITEM> <ITEM>Dan, judicium, vel judicans.</ITEM> <ITEM>Nephthali, conservavit me, vel
dilatavit me, vel certe implicuit
me.</ITEM> <ITEM>Gad, tentatio, sive latrunculus, vel
fortuna.</ITEM> <ITEM>Aser, beatitudo, sive beatus.</ITEM> <ITEM>Joseph, augmentum.</ITEM> <ITEM>Benjamin, filius dextrae.</ITEM> <ITEM>Rebecca, multa patientia, sive
multum accepit.</ITEM> <ITEM>Rachel, ovis, vel videns principium.</ITEM> <ITEM>Sephora, avis ejus, vel pulchritudo,
