ejus,</HI>  juxta Latinam Hieronymi versionem ejusdem
nominis.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Tiberivou, oJravsewû aujtwðn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Tracwnivtidoû, ejmporivaû luvph.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Fanouhúl, provswpon qeouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Farisaiðoû, diakecwrismevnoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1163"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>EN TW KATA IWANNHN EUAGGELIW.</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1163" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1163A"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Ainon</HI>  bene sonat  <HI REND="ITALIC">fontem virtutis;</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231163A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ain</HI>  enim
dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">fons,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231163A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">on, robur</HI>  ac  <HI REND="ITALIC">virtus.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijnwðh, phghú dunavmewû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1163" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1163A"></COL> In Lexico Origeniano consequenti Galilaea interpretatur
non  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpovkamyiû,</FOREIGN>  sed  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpokavluyiû</FOREIGN>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">revelatio,</HI> 
non  <HI REND="ITALIC">deflexus;</HI>  idque jure merito, nam Hebraeum
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231163B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">gala,</HI>  non tantum significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">migravit</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">in captivitatem
deportatus est;</HI>  verum etiam  <HI REND="ITALIC">revelavit</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">retexit.</HI> 
Itaque librariorum imperitia mutatum videtur vocabulum
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpokavluiyû</FOREIGN>  III  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpovkamyiû</FOREIGN> ; III ex accentu
satis liquet in codice ms. ubi legitur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpovkamyiû</FOREIGN></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Galilaiva,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="c" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1163" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1163B"></COL> Martian. ut ex ejus annotatione intelligis, legerat
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpovkamyiû,</FOREIGN>  ac verterat,  <HI REND="ITALIC">deflexus.</HI>  Postea emendavit
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpokavluyiû</FOREIGN>  ex uno Colbertino ms. nosque ita ex
Vaticano restituimus.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpokavluyiû, hÞ kulisthú,
hÞ metoikismovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Efrai0m, hujxhmevnoû, hÞ karpoforwðn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Kanaú, kthðsiû, hÞ ejkthvsato.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="d" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1163" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1163B"></COL> Pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Messivaû, hjleimmevnoû,</FOREIGN>  legerat pridem Martianaeus
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nazarevq hjlehmevnoû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Nazareth, misericordiam consecutus,</HI> 
ipsumque e regione titulum de Nazareth apposuerat
ex Hieronymo. Cumque statim alierum
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Nazareth</HI>  subsequatur, ita de priore in Notis hariolatus
 <COL N="1164B"></COL> est: Vocabuli  <HI REND="ITALIC">Nazareth</HI>  repetitio ac diversa ejusdem
interpretatio perspicue ostendit primo loco  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nazoraiðoû</FOREIGN> 
lectirandum. Christus enim  <HI REND="ITALIC">Nazaraeus</HI>  dictus
est, et a Deo prae participibus suis misericordiam
consecutus, uberiorem praestitit nobis gratiam atque
virtutem. Apud Hieronymum in Matthaeo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Nazareth</HI> 
habet omnia significata quae hic recensentur in propria
columna;  <HI REND="ITALIC">Nazoraeus</HI>  vero in eodem Matthaeo
 <HI REND="ITALIC">mundus</HI>  solummodo exprimitur. Postea a Colbertino
ms. admonitus  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Messivaû hjlhmevnoû</FOREIGN>  (pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>hjleimmevnoû</FOREIGN> ) rescribi
jussit, quemadmodum nos ex Vatic. emendamus.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Messivaû, hjleimmevnoû, hÞ Cristovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nazareúq, kaqarovthû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="5" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1163" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1163B"></COL> Verbo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Salim</HI>  manifestissime declaratur Graecum
 <COL N="1164A"></COL> Fragmentum libri Nominum ipsissimum esse contextum
quem Latinum sermonem convertit Hieronymus;
de Salim namque aiebat:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Saalim, pugilii, sive
volae, aut ortus aquarum, quod brevius Graece dicitur</HI> 
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>bruvonta.</FOREIGN>  Praeterea multis aliis argumentis probari potest
totum istud secundum Fragmentum vere Origenianum
esse, ut ex serie vocabulorum, quae eadem
est in Graeco saepius et in Latino Hieronymi contextu;
deinde ex iisdem omnino etymologiis ac similibus
interpretationibus, quas nunc curiosus Lector
conferre adinvicem nullo negotio potest, editionis nostrae
invitatus subsidio.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Saleiúm bruvonta.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sucaúr. suskiasmovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Silwaúm, ajpestalmevnoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="6" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1164" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1164A"></COL> Etymologiam vocabuli  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>qeoún</FOREIGN>  non legimus apud
Hieronymum in libro Nominum; prochveque judicium
est, hoc nomen additum fuisse librariorum
Graecorum temeritate, qua superius etiam ad fiderunt
Graeca nonnulla vocabula. Praeter haec autem
quae de illo nomine  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>qeoún,</FOREIGN>  hic afferuntur, aliquid scribere
juvabit. Alii igitur dictum volunt  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qeoún, ajpoú touð
qeaðsqai</FOREIGN>  quod omnia intueatur et cernat. Alii a nomine
 <COL N="1164B"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>devoû,</FOREIGN>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">metus,</HI>  quod sit formidabilis. Plerique
 <COL N="1165"></COL> cum nostro codice Regio,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpov touð Qevein</FOREIGN>  quod
est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">a currendo.</HI>  Existimarunt enim olim multi, sidera
 <COL N="1166"></COL> et stellas, quae perpetuo cursu feruntur, esse deos.
Consule Mythologos Scriptores.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qeoún, ajpoú touð qevein kaiú peritrevcein,
 <COL N="1165"></COL> kaiú mhdamhð iJstavnai, ajllaú</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1165" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1165"></COL> Colbertin.,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajllaú plhrouðn,</FOREIGN>  absque voce  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pavnta.</FOREIGN></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pavnta
plhrouðn.</FOREIGN></ITEM></LIST></DIV2> <DIV2 SECOND=YES COM="1165"><HEAD COM="1165"><HI REND="ITALIC">Hieronymus.</HI> 
