eJbraièdi dialevktwé.</FOREIGN></HEAD> <COL N="1165"></COL> <LIST COM="1165"><ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adaúm</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1165" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1165A"></COL> Nomen  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231165A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Adam</HI>  in tot varias etymologias diffunditur
apud Graecos; quia illud confundunt cum
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Adama, Dam,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ada:</HI>  quorum primum  <HI REND="ITALIC">pulverem</HI>  et
 <HI REND="ITALIC">terram rubram</HI>  significat;  <HI REND="ITALIC">Dam</HI>  vero  <HI REND="ITALIC">sanguinem,</HI>  et
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Ada testimonium.</HI>  Sonat autem  <HI REND="ITALIC">carnem</HI>  per abusum,
quo Philo et Origenes saepius haud nominum significationes
exprimunt in vocabulis, sed rerum eventus,
aut Patriarcharum virtutes. Sic ab eis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Adam</HI>  dicitur
 <HI REND="ITALIC">terra cornea,</HI>  quia ex terra corpus Adae compactum
est. Noe alibi interpretantur etiam  <HI REND="ITALIC">justum,</HI>  propter
verba Scripturae ubi  <HI REND="ITALIC">justus</HI>  dicitur esse inventus.
Quod vero de nominibus  <HI REND="ITALIC">Adam</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">Noe</HI>  hic admonemas,
in aliis bene multis consequenter observandum
erit curioso Lectori; frequentes enim sunt apud memoratos
Graecos Scriptores hujusmodi interpretationes
allegoricae ac symbolicae. Consule etiam quae de
nomine Abel jam observata sunt in priori Fragmento.</NOTE> ,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ghð sarkoumevnhú, hÞ marturiva,
hÞ ghgenhúû, hÞ aænqrwpoû, hÞ couðû, hÞ
ghð ejruqraú, hÞ aißma, hÞ oJmoivwsiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abbaú, pathúr, hÞ pfevsbuû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abeúl, ajtmiúû, hÞ pevnqoû, hÞ ajnafevrwn, hÞ
scoivnisma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Kaviçn, kthðma, hÞ zhlotupiva.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1165" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1165A"></COL> Non desunt in Manuscripto Graeco Scriptoris errores,
 <COL N="1166A"></COL> nec corruptelae nominum; manifesta et confessa
vitia plerumque sine notis emendo; caetera
describo diligenter, prout scripta leguntur in codice
Graeco, V. g.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Siúq</FOREIGN>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Shúq,</FOREIGN>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aavred</FOREIGN> ; pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iavred</FOREIGN> ; et  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Enwðû</FOREIGN> 
pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nwðe.</FOREIGN></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Siúq, fuvteuma, hÞ ajnavstasiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Enwúû, ejpilanqanovmenoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Kaiçnaún, kthðsiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Malelehúl, ajntavllagma ijscurovn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1165" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1165A"></COL> Corrupte lectum a Martian.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Aared</FOREIGN>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iavred,</FOREIGN>  quod
emendat Vatic.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iavred, katavbasiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Enwúc, ejgkainismovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Maqousavla, ajpestalmevnoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1166" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1166A"></COL> Observa, Lector, non eodem modo interpretatum
fuisse semper Hieronymum nomina eadem Hebraica;
nam supra in libro Numerorum sic ab eo vertitur
nomen Sethi.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Seth, positio, sive posuit</HI>  De Lamecho
etiam haec habet in Luca:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Lamech, humilis:
quidam putant percutientem, sive percussum posse resonare.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Lavmec, eujgnwvmwn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1165" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1165A"></COL> Iterum corrupte Martian. ex Regio  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Enwðû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Enos</HI> 
substituerat pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nwðe</FOREIGN>  nomine. Castigatur a Vaticano.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nwðe, ajnavpausiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Shúm, tevleioû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Caúm, tolmhroúû, hÞ propethvû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iavfeq,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1166" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1166A"></COL> Corrupte in manuscripto Regio scriptum est
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>platuvswmoû</FOREIGN>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>platusmoúû</FOREIGN>  quod significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">dilatationem.</HI> 
In aliis permultis nominibus omittuntur
aliquot litterae, spiritus et accentus; quorum omnium
defectum bona fide, nec oscitanter supplere
curavi.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>platuvswmoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Arfaxaúd, profhteiva oJrwmevnh.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="c" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1166" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1166A"></COL> In Vatican.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Salevq</FOREIGN> : tum additur post  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diavbasiû</FOREIGN>  vocem,
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eJbraiðoû, hÞ gnwðmh.</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Hebraeus, aut sententia,</HI>  quae
quidem quo huc pacto referantur, non video.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Saleúd, ajpestalmevnoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ebeúr, diaperwðn, hÞ diavbasiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="1167"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Nacwúr, desmhú oJrathú ejrcomevnh.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Esrwúm,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1167" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1167A"></COL> Hieronymus hanc etymologiam  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>devhsiû ejscathú,</FOREIGN> 
id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">obsecratio novissima,</HI>  copulavit. cum vocabulo
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Nachor.</HI>  Graeci autem cum nomine  <HI REND="ITALIC">Esrom.</HI>  Quare
ne suspensus haereat hoc loco Lector, scire debet
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231167A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">athar,</HI>  idem esse quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">deprecari;</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231167A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ahharon,</HI> 
significare  <HI REND="ITALIC">postremum,</HI>  seu  <HI REND="ITALIC">novissimum.</HI>  Hinc
ergo Hieronymus Graecorum opinionem secutus, ni
fallor, in etymologia nominis Nachoris, illud derivavit
ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231167B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ahar;</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">athar,</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231167B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  Similiter dicendum
de  <HI REND="ITALIC">Esrom.</HI>  qui apud Graecos interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">obsecratio,</HI> 
seu  <HI REND="ITALIC">deprecatio postrema.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>devhsiû ejscavth.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qaraú, eujwdiva bradeiða.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Abram, pathúr uJyhloúû, hÞ peravthû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abraaúm, pathúr uJyivstou, hÞ pathúr
ejqnwðn, hÞ pathúr oijktirmwðn, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1167" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1167A"></COL> Conferendus hic venit Origenes lib. I contra
Celsum num. 45. ubi Abraham exponitur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pathúr
ejklektoúû thðû hjkouvshû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">pater electus sonitus.</HI>  Et Clemeus
Alexandr. Strom. l. V, p. 400:  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abraaúm
eJrmhneuvetai pathúr ejklektoúû hjcouðû,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Abraham exponitur
pater electus sonitus.</HI>  Uterque autem a Philone
interpretationem desumpserit, qui lib. de Gigantibus
p. 228:  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Kaleiðtai pathúr ejklektoúû hjcouðû eJrmhneuqeiúû
1Abraaúm oJ touð spoudaivou logismoúû× ejxeilegmevnoû gaúr
 <COL N="1168A"></COL> kaiú kekaqarmevnoû kaiú pathúr fwnhðû, hßé sunhcouðmen.</FOREIGN> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Interpretatur Abraham pater electus soni, id est, intellectus
probi viri. Est enim electus et purgatus, et pater
vocis qua consonamus.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pathúr
ejklektovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Isaaúk, gevlwû, kaiú carav.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Iakwúb, pternisthúû, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1168" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1168A"></COL> Martianaeus ex Regio  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eæscatoû</FOREIGN>  legerat, et Latin.
 <HI REND="ITALIC">postremus.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eæscata kratwðn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Hsauð, liqolovgion, hÞ poivhma,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1167" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1167A"></COL> Genus est arboris, quae dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">robur,</HI>  unde  <HI REND="ITALIC">roboreus.</HI> 
