 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>LEXIKON.</FOREIGN></HEAD> <COL N="1203"></COL> <LIST COM="1203"><CAPTION COM="1203"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>A</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adaúm, ghgenhúû, aænqrwpoû, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203A"></COL> Omissa est vox  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ghð</FOREIGN>  in manuscripto, quam supplet
superius Fragmentum Graecum libri nominum
Hebraicorum. Praeterea confundit Philo, vel Origenes
Hebraicum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231203A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Adam,</HI>  cum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231203A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Ada,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231203A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Ud,</HI> 
unde eliciunt etymologias,  <HI REND="ITALIC">venturae,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">venientes</HI>  et
 <HI REND="ITALIC">testificationis.</HI>  Non sic doctus Hieronymus, qui nomen
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Ada</HI>  ex Hebraeo  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231203B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Ud</HI>  interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">testimonium;
Adam</HI>  vero  <HI REND="ITALIC">terram rubram</HI>  ex themate  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231203B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Adam,</HI>  cum  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aleph,</HI>  quod significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">rubere, et rufum
esse.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejruqraú,
hÞ ghð sarkoumevnh, hÞ ghð ejrcomevnh,
hÞ marturiva.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203B"></COL> Rescribendum  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adavma,</FOREIGN>  nullus dubito. Concinit
porro Josephus Anti. C. I, et Hesychius, qui
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>parqenikhún,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">virginalem</HI>  interpretatur, Plinius  <HI REND="ITALIC">terram
virginem</HI>  ab Esubope inventam narrat lib. XXXIII, 3;
Scholiastes Apollonii ad II, 874, Samum insulam
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Parqenivan</FOREIGN>  vocat.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adaúm, ghð parqevnoû, hÞ ghð aiJmatwvdhû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203A"></COL> De interpretatione hac Graeca nominis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abel</HI> 
plura diximus in primo superiori Fragmento. Consule
scholia nostra.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abeúl, ajpoú qeouð, hÞ ajnafevrwn ejpiú
qeoún, hÞ ajtmiúû, hÞ pevnqoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abraaúm, pathúr uJyivstou, hÞ pathúr
uiJouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203A"></COL> Violenter omnino interpretantur Graeci nomen
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Aaron,</HI>  quod proprie  <HI REND="ITALIC">montanum</HI>  sonat, sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">montem
fortitudinis.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aarwún, kibwtoúû, hÞ oJrwðn oJravseiû, hÞ
lovgoû, hÞ uåyoû, hÞ dunatoúû, hÞ pneuðma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abdiouð, douðlovû</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203B"></COL> Hieron. legisse videtur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qeouð,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Dei,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Domini:</HI> 
 <COL N="1204B"></COL> certe longe rectius.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>mou,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203A"></COL> In voce  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abdiou</HI>  etymologiam  <HI REND="ITALIC">confitentis</HI>  inveniunt
interpretes Graecorum, quia apud ipsos saepius
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abiouúd</FOREIGN>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abia,</HI>  et  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abdiouúd</FOREIGN>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abdia</HI>  corrupte
legebatur. Unde suspicabantur hoc nomen formatum
 <COL N="1203B"></COL> ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231203C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">jada, confessus est.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejxomologhtovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abenevzer, laoúû bohqoúû, hÞ livqoû touð
bohqouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abesaloúm, patroúû eijrhvnhû ejp1 aujtouvû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="5" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1203" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1203B"></COL> De nomine  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aminadab,</HI>  pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abinadab</HI>  diximus
in scholiis nostris. Sed hic observandum, Graecos
interpretari illud vocabulum sub utraque forma
corrupta et integra;  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aminadab</HI>  enim dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">populus
 <COL N="1204A"></COL> desiderabilis; et Abinadab, patris mei beneplacitum,</HI>  id
est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">propensa voluntas.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aminadaúb,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="c" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1204" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1204B"></COL> Ipsa hujus nominis prior expositio rescribendum
persuadet  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abinadaúb</FOREIGN> ; notumque praeterea est
passim in Graecis mss.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>b</FOREIGN>  et  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>m</FOREIGN>  confundi. Vatic. etiam
in postremo  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>b</FOREIGN>  peccat, legens  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aminadaúm,</FOREIGN>  quod et
subsequenti  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abigavhn</FOREIGN>  postponit.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>patrovû mou eujdokiva, hÞ
eæqnoû ejpiqumhtovn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="6" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1204" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1204A"></COL> Ex etymologia subjecta facile intelligitur scriptorem
Graecum pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abigal,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abigail,</HI>  legisse
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Abigaen.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abigavhn, patrovû mou ajpokavluyiû,
hÞ drovsoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="d" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1204" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1204B"></COL> Vatic. duobus verbis  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>taú nuðn.</FOREIGN>  Sunt porro qui
 <COL N="1205B"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Apfouúû</FOREIGN>  legant ex Hebraeo  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231205A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> , quo cognomento
Jonathas frater Judae Machabaei appellatus est
I Mach. II, 5.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Apfwú,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="7" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1204" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1204A"></COL> Hoc nomen legitur apud LXX, IV Reg. II, 14,
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Pouð oJ Qeoúû 1Hliouú 1Affwú</FOREIGN> ;  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ubi Deus Eliu Appho?</HI> 
Quod Hieronymus Latine reddidit ex Heb.  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231204A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">aph-hu,</HI>  vel,  <HI REND="ITALIC">aph-o, etiamnunc?</HI>  vel forte ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231204A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">aphpho,</HI>  quod LXX et Hieronymus legisse videntur
in Hebraeorum exemplaribus. Occurrit enim illud
adverbium etiam cum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231204B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  in fine, ut Gen. XXVII, 33,
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231204B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,  <HI REND="ITALIC">mi appho hu,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">quis nunc ille</HI> 
pro  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231204B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">aph-hu</HI>  hodierno. Itaque suspicari
possumus lectum fuisse antiquitus, in aliquot
saltem codicibus, nomen  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231204C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">aphpho,</HI>  sive  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpfwú,</FOREIGN> 
in Graeco Origeniano Lexico etiamnum superstes.
Cui autem ascribenda sit sequens annotatiuncula,
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ouåtwû eußron,</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">sic inveni,</HI>  an Origeni admonenti  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpfwú</FOREIGN> 
se invenisse scriptum in Hebraeo, an Graeco scriptori,
 <COL N="1204B"></COL> qui indicare voluerit etymologiam nominis
non a seipso confictam, sed ab alio accepisse descriptam
ac describendam, Lectori judicandum permitto.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>tanuðn, ajrtivwû× ouåtwû eußron.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="1205"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abraaúm, pathúr ejklektoúû, pathúr
uiJwðn, hÞ peratikoúû, hÞ pathúr uJyivstou,
hÞ pathúr</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205A"></COL> Apud Suidam  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajpovbleptoû</FOREIGN>  dicitur homo  <HI REND="ITALIC">conspicuus,</HI> 
et  <HI REND="ITALIC">celeber:</HI>  quod maxime convenit Abrahae,
fama ac virtute praeclarissimo;  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>bleptoúû</FOREIGN>  igitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">conspicuum</HI> 
reddo: sed nihil mutans in voce Graeca
ms. codicis.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>bleptovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abeirwún, pathúr uJyhlovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aggaiðoû, eJortaiú, hÞ eJortazovntwn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Agrivppaû, sustrevfwn ejxaivfnhû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205A"></COL> Unde corruptum nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Agchus</HI>  sumptum hic
legatur, nondum invenire potui, nec conjecturis
assequi.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Agcouúû, ajpovpluma,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205B"></COL> Contrario sensu in Vatic. est  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejpikavlumma,</FOREIGN>  quod
 <COL N="1206B"></COL> est  <HI REND="ITALIC">operculum, velamen,</HI>  etc.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>hÞ ajpokavlumma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aermwðn, fwðû metevwron, hÞ pneuðma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Azahúl, ejpifanhúû, oårasiû, hÞ kravtoû, hÞ
ijscuúû qeouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Azhkaú, sterevwsiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aqhðnai,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205A"></COL> Multum depravata legitur etymologia isthaec in
ms. Regio codice Graeco: scribit enim  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>kaiú rwð diaskezovmenai,</FOREIGN> 
quod nihil significat. Separata consociavi,
omissa supplevi, et accentum vitiosum sustuli,
monitus ab Hieronymi interpretatione, quam violentam
et sibi alienam declaravit supra in collectione
nominuam ex Actibus Apostolicis. De nominibus
duobus consequentibus consule quae diximus ibi in
Notis.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>kairwðé</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="b" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1206" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1206B"></COL> Vatic. quoque ms.,  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diaskezovmenai.</FOREIGN></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>diaskedazovmenai.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aiæguptoû, skotasmovû, hÞ qlivyiû, hÞ paroikiva.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijqiopiva, tapeivnwsiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijlamivtai, parablevponteû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abbaú, pathvr.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abdemevlec, douleuvwn basileið.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Acra, diastrofhú,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205A"></COL> Confundunt Graeci  <HI REND="ITALIC">Achar</HI>  cum vocabulo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Bechar,</HI> 
sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">Bechor,</HI>  quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">primogenitum</HI>  sonat.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>prwtovtokoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Acikaúm, ajdelfovû mou ajnavstasiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Acimevlec, ajdelfovû mou basilevwû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Acwúr, diastrofhv.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Awúq, ejxomologhtoúû, aiænesiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abennhúr, patroúû luvcnoû, hÞ uiJouð luvcnoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="5" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205A"></COL> Pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abia</HI>  apud Graecos scriptum forte erat alicubi
 <COL N="1205B"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Abiar,</HI>  unde formatum volebant ex  <HI REND="ITALIC">ab pater,</HI> 
et ex  <HI REND="ITALIC">bar, filius.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abiva, patroúû, hÞ pathúr uiJoúû, hÞ patrovû
mou.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abiavqar,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="c" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1206" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1206B"></COL> Pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>kenovn</FOREIGN>  in Vatic.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>hðn</FOREIGN>  scribitur.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>kenovn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="6" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1205" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1205B"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Abiezri</HI>  legimus supra in contextu Hieronymiano
 <COL N="1206A"></COL> pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abiezer,</HI>  quod male interpretatum dicebamus,
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Dei mei auxilium,</HI>  cum  <HI REND="ITALIC">abi</HI>  sonet  <HI REND="ITALIC">patris mei.</HI> 
Caeterum etymologia Graeca cum vocativo casu
exprimitur:  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejklovgistoû</FOREIGN>  vero est qui subductis rationibus
expendit, perpendit et aestimat, vel numeratum
dat aliquid, Th.  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejklogivzomai.</FOREIGN>  Sed quomodo Hebraeum
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231206A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">abiezer</HI>  respondeat Graeco  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ejklovgiste,</FOREIGN> 
nondum intellexi. Respexerunt forsitan Graeci ad
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231206B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">aza,</HI>  quod utique magis accedit ad vocem Graecam,
quam  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231206B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">azar.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Abiavzer, patroúû ejklovgiste.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abimevlec, patroúû basileiva, hÞ pathúr
basileuvû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abiouúd, pathúr prosercovmenoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Agaboû, aæggeloû,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="7" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1206" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1206A"></COL> Forte legendum in Graeco  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eujavggeloû</FOREIGN>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eujaggelismovû.</FOREIGN> 
Id saltem indicare videtur Hieronymi interpretatio,
quam ipse ut violentam abjicit.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>eujaggelismovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Agaúg, dwðma, hÞ movscoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Agar, paroivkhsiû, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="8" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1206" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1206A"></COL> Hebraeum verbum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231206C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">agar,</HI>  cum significet  <HI REND="ITALIC">colligere</HI> 
et  <HI REND="ITALIC">congerere,</HI>  ex aequo respondet Graeco  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>sustrevfw,</FOREIGN> 
id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">convolvo in fascem,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">colligo. Agar</HI>  igitur
quae Graece interpretatur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>sustrevfousa,</FOREIGN>  Latine exprimi
debet  <HI REND="ITALIC">congregans,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">colligens.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>sustrevfousa,
hÞ proshvlutoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="9" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1206" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1206A"></COL> Nomen illud Graecum non interpretatur Hieronymus.
 <COL N="1206B"></COL> Unde autem  <HI REND="ITALIC">opus,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">officium</HI>  sonet, non
possum conjectura prorsus assequi, nisi ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231206D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hhag,</HI> 
quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">sacrificium</HI>  festi significat, Graeci illud nomen
 <COL N="1207A"></COL> figuratum suspicentur.  <HI REND="ITALIC">Sermo</HI>  autem dici potest,
quasi sit ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231207A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">aga,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">hegeh,</HI>  hoc est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">loquela</HI> 
et  <HI REND="ITALIC">meditatione,</HI>  aut  <HI REND="ITALIC">gemitu.</HI>  Denique Hebraeum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231207B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">namal</HI>  violenter accedit ad vocem Graecam  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>aæggeloû,</FOREIGN> 
ut ex affinitate  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>g,</FOREIGN>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">n,</HI>  et ex  <HI REND="ITALIC">l</HI>  medium habeat significantiam
 <HI REND="ITALIC">circumcisionis.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Aggeloû, eærgon, hÞ peritomhú, hÞ lovgoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><COL N="1207"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Ader, eujprevpeia.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adrivaû, sunochú kakwðn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Adonai0, kuvrioû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1207" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1207A"></COL> Puto non  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aza,</HI>  hoc loco legendum cum Graecis,
sed  <HI REND="ITALIC">Achaz,</HI>  vel saltem  <HI REND="ITALIC">Oza:</HI>  quia in illis vocibus invenitur,
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231207C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ahh,</HI>  quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">fratrem</HI>  sonat: et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231207C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Uza</HI>  vel
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Oza,</HI>  quod exprimitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">fortitudo ejus.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Azaú, ijscuúû aujthðû, hÞ ajdelfovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Azarivaû, Bohvqeia kurivou, hÞ ejpistrofhú
kurivou.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Azwtoû, ijscuúû, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1207" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1207A"></COL> Monet supra hoc modo Hieronymus Lectorem:
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Azolus,</HI>  inquit,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Hebraice vocatur</HI>  ESDOD,  <HI REND="ITALIC">et secundum
pristinum nomen etymologiam habet ignis patruelis.</HI> 
Hoc est, ni fallor, Graece expressum in voce
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>purgevneia,</FOREIGN>  quam sic scriptam relinquo, ne criticos
quosdam provocem ad jurgia: licet non me fugiat
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>gevneia</FOREIGN>  plur. num. saepe sumptum pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>gevneion,</FOREIGN> 
quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">mentum,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">barbam</HI>  significat. Sed ut  <HI REND="ITALIC">ignis
patruelis</HI>  intelligatur juxta Hieronymum, scribendum
 <COL N="1207B"></COL> erit  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>puðr gevnnaû,</FOREIGN>  hoc est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">ignis avunculus.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>purgevneia.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijdeúm, aiJmavtwsiû, hÞ trufhv.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijlaúm, oJdoúû, hÞ provpulon, hÞ stoav.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Akeldamaú, cwrivon aiåmatoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Akariúm, kthnotrofeiðon.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Akravbin, skorpivoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Alevxandroû, eæqnwn sunochv.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Allovfuloi, pivptonteû</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1208" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1208A"></COL> Allophyli sunt  <HI REND="ITALIC">Philisthiim,</HI>  quos  <HI REND="ITALIC">cadentes poculo</HI> 
interpretatur S. Hieronymus ex Heb.  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231208A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">naphal,</HI> 
et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231208A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">sethi.</HI>  Graeci ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231208A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">naphal,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231208A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">nephes,</HI>  id
est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">cadavere.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ptwvmati</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="a" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1207" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1207B"></COL> Nihilosecius, quandoquidem Hieronymus continuo
vertit  <HI REND="ITALIC">cadentes poculo:</HI>  pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ptwvmati,</FOREIGN>  quod
est  <HI REND="ITALIC">cadavere,</HI>  lectum arbitror ab illo  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>povmati,</FOREIGN>  quod
est  <HI REND="ITALIC">poculo,</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">potione;</HI>  sicque olim rectius scriptum
a Graecis.</NOTE> .</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Alfaioúû, oijcovmenoû, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1208" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1208A"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ciloúû</FOREIGN>  cum accentu gravi  <HI REND="ITALIC">pabulum</HI>  significat;
in manuscripto autem penacuitur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>civloû.</FOREIGN>  Videtur
positum hoc modo pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>cilivoi</FOREIGN>  aut pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ciliostoúû,</FOREIGN>  quod
sonat  <HI REND="ITALIC">millesimum.</HI>  Cui conjecturae astipulatur interpretatio
Hieronymi, et verbum Hebraeum  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231208B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">alaph,</HI> 
vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">elaph,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">mille,</HI>  sive etiam  <HI REND="ITALIC">docuit.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>civloû touð aujtouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Amalhúk, laoúû ejkleivcwn, hÞ ejkklivnwn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Ambakouúm, paravlhyiû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aminadaúm, eæqnoû ejpiqumhtoún, hÞ laoúû
