in Origeniano Lexico.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Euøa, mhthúr zwvntwn, eujprevpeia.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1261"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Z</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zaraú, ejpifanhúû, ajnatolhv.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zacarivaû, nikhthúû, hÞ</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1261" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1261A"></COL> Confunditur hic nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zacharias</HI>  cum nomine
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Ischarioth,</HI>  quod interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">memoria mortis.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>mnhvmh qanavtou.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zorobabeúl, mikroúû ajpoú sugcuvsewû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zebedaiðoû, qusiva mou aujthv.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zakcaioúû, keklhrwmevnoû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zaboulwún, cavrisma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="5" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1261" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1261A"></COL> Nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zelpha</HI>  interpretatur  <HI REND="ITALIC">ambulans os,</HI>  illud
enim sumptum Veteres volunt ab  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231261A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">azal, abiit,</HI> 
etc. et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231261A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">phe,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">os,</HI>  ab ore, non ab osse.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zevlfa, ejxavntlhma.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="6" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1261" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1261A"></COL> Opposui vocabulo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zan</HI>  nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zanoe,</HI>  quia in illo
est syllaba  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zan.</HI>  Forte  <HI REND="ITALIC">Zenam</HI>  legi potuisset. Caeterum
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Zan</HI>  verbum Graecum est, non Hebraicum nomen.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zaðn, zhðé.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1261"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>H</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Hsaièaû,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="7" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1261" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1261A"></COL> S. Hieronymus in Nominibus ex Epistola ad Romanos
monuit nos, tria nomina, quae sequuntur,
 <COL N="1261B"></COL> cum H esse legenda,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Hesaias, Helias, Herodionem.</HI> 
Ne mireris igitur, quod haec cum aspiratione legantur
 <COL N="1262A"></COL> a nobis juxta spiritum asperum in Graeco notatum.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>skiasmoúû, hÞ eæparsiû qeouð.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Hliouð, qeoúû ijscurouð aujqevnthû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Hsauð, davsoû, hÞ druvû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>3Hl, qeovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>5Hq, zwðn.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="8" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1262" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1262A"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Divinus ignis</HI>  non est interpretatio nominis  <HI REND="ITALIC">Eliae,</HI> 
sed virtutis ejus, aut zeli divini, quo flagrabat, significatio.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>2Hlivaû, qeiðon puðr.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1261"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Q</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qamaúr,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="9" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1262" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1262A"></COL> Etymologiam hoc loco prorsus depravatam docuit
nos superius exemplar Graecum col. 631, ubi
nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Thamar</HI>  interpretatur  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qeouð lovgoi</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Dei sermones,</HI> 
vel  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>deuðro lovgoi</FOREIGN>   <HI REND="ITALIC">huc sermones.</HI>  Lege itaque  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>deuðro
lovgoi</FOREIGN>  et  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>kuvrioû.</FOREIGN></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>deuðloge, hÞ kuvri.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qemaú, ajnatolhú.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qekeúl, mevtron.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="10" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1262" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1262A"></COL> An pro  <HI REND="ITALIC">Tabrathen</HI>  legendum sit  <HI REND="ITALIC">Thartan,</HI>  non
satis mihi compertum:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Chabrath</HI>  opposui propter
