Philo: ex Hebraeo namque  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Abraham,</HI>  vix
excutitur,  <HI REND="ITALIC">pater electus soni,</HI>  nisi subintelligat,  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">ab,</HI>  partem significare,  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">bar,</HI>  electum, et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hama,</HI> 
sonitum. Hieronymi similiter interpretatio magis
vera comprobatur secundum sonum litterarum, quam
juxta fidem elementorum; vox etenim  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abraham</HI> 
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  quinque tantum litteris scripta est:  <HI REND="ITALIC">pater</HI> 
autem  <HI REND="ITALIC">videns populum</HI>  septem habet elementa,  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> ,
ita ut  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ab,</HI>  sit  <HI REND="ITALIC">pater;</HI>   <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">ra, videns,</HI>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231281D" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> 
 <HI REND="ITALIC">am, populus.</HI>  Aliam interpretationem nominis Abraham
videsis libro Quaest. Hebr. in Genesim.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pathúr ejklektoúû hjcouðû,</FOREIGN>  p.
292,</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Abiouúd,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1281" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1281A"></COL> In expositione multorum nominum Philo nonnulla
supplet, sive subintelligit elementa non expressa:
hic in  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abiud</HI>  abjicit syllabam ultimam  <HI REND="ITALIC">ud:</HI> 
nam  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abi</HI>  solum dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">pater meus.</HI>  Non sic Hieronymus,
qui nomen integrum exprimit, dicendo  <HI REND="ITALIC">patris
robur;</HI>  sed insuper nomen illud accipit, ac si
corrupte scriberetur  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abiam,</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">Abium,</HI>  cum  <HI REND="ITALIC">m</HI>  in
fine, sicut in aliquot exemplaribus Italicae Versionis
 <COL N="1281B"></COL> S. Matthaei scriptum reperi  <HI REND="ITALIC">Eliam</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">Elium</HI>  pro
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Eliud. Abiam</HI>  porro significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">patrem eorum,</HI>  propter
 <HI REND="ITALIC">m</HI>  ultimam litteram, quae apud Hebraeos affixa est,
 <COL N="1282A"></COL> seu pronomen tertiae personae masculinae pluralis.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pathvr mou,</FOREIGN>  p. 414.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijleiúm, pulwðneû eJrmhneuvontai,</FOREIGN>  p.
477.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Aijqiopiva, tapeivnwsiû,</FOREIGN>  p. 53.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Agaúr,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="3" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1282" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1282A"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Paroivkhsiû</FOREIGN>  magis significat  <HI REND="ITALIC">commorationem</HI>  apud
exteros, quam  <HI REND="ITALIC">inquilinum,</HI>  qui  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>pavroikoû</FOREIGN>  dicitur, non
 <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>paroivkhsiû,</FOREIGN>  ut putavit novus Interpres.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>paroivkhsiû,</FOREIGN>  p. 427.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Amalhúk, laoúû ejkleivcwn,</FOREIGN>  p. 410 et
432.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Annaú,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="4" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1282" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1282A"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Anna</HI>  interpretatur Hieronymus  <HI REND="ITALIC">gratiam ejus,</HI> 
quia nomen terminatur per  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">He,</HI>  suffixum femininum
3 pers. sing.  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT> . Philo confundit  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hhen,</HI>  et
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hhanna;</HI>  et ideo affixum non exprimit.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>cavriû,</FOREIGN>  p. 294 et 602.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Aarwún</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="5" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1282" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1282A"></COL> Bene Philo nomen  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aaron</HI>  transtulit  <HI REND="ITALIC">montanum.</HI> 
Idque magis probare mihi visus est Hieronymus supra
in contextu ad vocem  <HI REND="ITALIC">Magdalene,</HI>  de qua ait:
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Sed melius sicut a monte montanus, ita turrensis a
turri dicatur.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ojreinovû,</FOREIGN>  p. 259.</ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="6" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1282" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1282A"></COL> Perplexa et intricata nimis etymologia nominis
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">Arphaxad</HI>  apud Philonem. Forte eam desumit
a verbo  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">hharaph,</HI>  et a nomine  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">sod,</HI>  vel
 <HI REND="ITALIC">sad,</HI>  quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">miseriam</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">calamitatem</HI>  sonat, sicut verbum
 <HI REND="ITALIC">Araph, provocavit,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">irritavit.</HI>  Potest etiam
oriri ex  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>  et  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231282C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">raphas,</HI>  id est,  <HI REND="ITALIC">calcando perturbavit.</HI></NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1Arfaxaút, oÝ eJrmhneuvetai sunetavraxe
talaipwrivan.</FOREIGN>  p. 1074.</ITEM> <ITEM><NOTE N="7" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1282" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1282B"></COL> <HI REND="ITALIC">Aunan,</HI>  confundit Philo cum  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aenaim,</HI>  vel  <HI REND="ITALIC">Aenan,</HI> 
quod oculos significat.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Auønan, ojfqalmoiú eJrmhneuvetai,</FOREIGN>  p.
413.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>1AmorjrJaiðoi, lalouðnteû,</FOREIGN>  p. 104.</ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1281"><COL N="1283"></COL> <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>B</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Babulwún, suvgcusiû,</FOREIGN>  p. 320.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Babulwún, metavqesiû,</FOREIGN>  p. 293.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Balaúk,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="1" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1283" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1283A"></COL> Pessime legitur in contextu Graeco Philonis  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ojnouúû</FOREIGN> 
pro  <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>aænouû,</FOREIGN>  quod nos restituimus in hac collectione
nominum. Sed quomodo  <HI REND="ITALIC">Balac</HI>  nomen interpretatum
sit  <HI REND="ITALIC">amens,</HI>  non proclivis est responsio. Si ad linguam
Arabicam liceat divertere,  <HI REND="ITALIC">Balac</HI>  haud incongrue
dicitur  <HI REND="ITALIC">amens;</HI>  quia  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231283A" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">balach,</HI>  sive  <HI REND="ITALIC">beleh</HI> 
Arabice est  <HI REND="ITALIC">moria, stultitia, fatuitas,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">insania.</HI>  Hieronymus
respexit ad  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231283B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">lacac,</HI>  seu  <HI REND="ITALIC">liccec,</HI>  quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">linxit</HI> 
et  <HI REND="ITALIC">lambit</HI>  significat; et ad  <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231283B" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">balle</HI>  idem sonans,
quod  <HI REND="ITALIC">atterere,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">consumere,</HI>  etc.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>aænouû,</FOREIGN>  p. 329.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Baqouhúl, qugavthr qeouð,</FOREIGN>  p. 157.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Balaaúm, mavtaioû laovû.</FOREIGN></ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Bavlla,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1283" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1283A"></COL> Hanc etymologiam Philo desumit ex Hebraeo
 <ORNAMENT IMAGE="0231283C" TEXT="[Hebrew Text]"></ORNAMENT>   <HI REND="ITALIC">bala,</HI>  cum  <HI REND="ITALIC">Ain</HI>  in fine, quod est Latine  <HI REND="ITALIC">absorbuit,
deglutivit.</HI>  Hieronymus vero vocem ipsam femininam
