semper eadem nomina Hebraica; nam  <HI REND="ITALIC">Liam</HI> 
pag. 410 dicit significare  <HI REND="ITALIC">fatigationem,</HI>  seu  <HI REND="ITALIC">laborantem,</HI> 
pag. autem 1084,  <HI REND="ITALIC">renuentem,</HI>  et  <HI REND="ITALIC">laborantem</HI>  simul.
 <COL N="1286A"></COL> Hanc interpretandi rationem imitatur saepius Hieronymus,
ut in promptu probatio est ex iisdem nominibus
in Genesi, ver. gr. et in Matthaeo recensitis,
de quibus supra diximus haud incuriosa.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Leiva, ajnaneuomevnh kaiú kopiwðsa,</FOREIGN>  p.
410 et 1084.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Lwút, eJrmhneuvetai ajpovklisiû,</FOREIGN>  p.
410.</ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1285"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>M</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Madiaúm, ejk krivsewû ojnomavzetai,</FOREIGN>  p.
