eo expositum libro de Mutatione Nominum, et alibi
saepius. Consule Hieronymum libro Hebraicarum
Quaestionum in Gen. cap. XVII, ubi castigat errorem
Graecorum, qui super nomine Sarae mutato
multas ineptias suspicantur.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>ajrchv mou,</FOREIGN>  p. 109 et 1058.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>SavrjrJa. aærcousa,</FOREIGN>  p. 109 et 1058.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sepfwvra, ojrnivqion,</FOREIGN>  p. 498.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Suceúm, wømoû,</FOREIGN>  p. 1074.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Suriva, eJrmhneuvetai metevwra,</FOREIGN>  p. 64.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sivkima, wjmivasiû eJrmhneuvetai,</FOREIGN>  p. 65.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sattiún, aækanqai,</FOREIGN>  p. 578.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Souúr, teiðcoû, hÞ eujqusmovû,</FOREIGN>  p. 479.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sumewún, eijsakohv,</FOREIGN>  p. 1060.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Sennaaúr, ejktinagmovû,</FOREIGN>  p. 330.</ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1289"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Q</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qavmar, eJrmhneuvetai foivnix,</FOREIGN>  p. 313.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Qamnaú, eækleiyiû saleuomevnh,</FOREIGN>  p. 432.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>QavrjrJa, eJrmhneuvetai kataskophú ojdmhðû,</FOREIGN> p. 572.</ITEM> <CAPTION COM="1289"><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Z</FOREIGN></CAPTION> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zaboulwún,</FOREIGN> <NOTE N="2" TYPE="foot" ANCHORED=Y COM="1289" BLOCK="NO"><COL N="1289A"></COL> De hac etymologia ita disserit Hieronymus lib.
Quaest. Hebr. in Genesim:  <HI REND="ITALIC">Male igitur et violenter
in libro Nominum Zabulon fluxus noctis interpretatur.</HI> 
Quaecumque ergo reperiuntur male ac violenter
interpretata in libro Nominum Hebraicorum, non
Hieronymo, sed Philoni, vel Origeni referenda
sunt.

Haec sunt quae ex Philonis Operibus colligere potui
nomina Hebraea. Si qua sint alia, fateor illa
 <COL N="1290A"></COL> effugisse diligentiam meam; atque veniam precor
ab erudito benignoque lectore, propter omissas forte,
non omisso animo, dictiones nonnullas, quas
ipse studio feliciori supplendas curabit. Ex iis porro,
quae modo attulimus, facile liquet quanta sit
concordia, vel dissonantia Philonis ab Hieronymo.
Nolim tamen asserere Philonem nihil aliud scripsisse
in libro Hebraicorum Nominum, praeter illas
interpretationes vocabulorum, quae passim occurrunt
legenti in editis ejusdem Opusculis. Quare ut
manifestius appareant tam Philonis etymologiae,
quam Hieronymi castigationes et adjectiones, Graeca
Fragmenta libri Nominum supra exhibuimus,
Latine a nobis reddita, atque comparata cum Hieronymianis
interpretationibus, uti actitatum est in
praecedenti collectione tribus columnis distincta.</NOTE>   <FOREIGN LANG=Greek>fuvsiû nukterivaû ojnomavzetai,</FOREIGN> 
p. 1113.</ITEM> <ITEM><FOREIGN LANG=Greek>Zevlfa, poreuovmenon stovma,</FOREIGN>  p. 428.</ITEM></LIST></DIV1> <DIV1 SECOND=YES COM="1289"><HEAD COM="1289"><HI REND="ITALIC">Hieronymus.</HI></HEAD> <COL N="1282"></COL> <LIST COM="1282"><CAPTION COM="1282">A</CAPTION> <ITEM>Adam, homo, sive terrenus, aut
